+# Agent Note: Edit a human request into an independent replay
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-11-02-edit-and-replay.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Continuing after a completed answer cannot evaluate a revised earlier request without carrying its original answer into the model context. Replacing a stored event would change the original record and could misrepresent external actions already performed. Assessment extensions also need to bind a new chat to existing request limits before its first step runs.
+
+## Decision
+
+The existing fork request accepts an optional replay anchor and revised text. The Host observes the source without activating its agent, requires an original human append, and copies only the prefix before that message. The existing fork algorithm closes any open turn or tool boundary. The replacement receives a fresh message identity and the stored non-text content, including attachment references. The browser cannot provide replacement attachment references.
+
+An awaited `api-session/forked` event runs after child creation and workspace attachment, before replay admission. Extensions can persist assessment membership and reject admission when that persistence fails. The event also covers ordinary forks. This operation-local extension point does not add source metadata to native Session logs; [independent fork semantics](../architecture/2026-10-23-independent-session-forks.md) remain intact.
+
+The Chat owner supplies a callback through the existing props and injection contracts. Its accessible editor keeps the original message visible, retains drafts on failure, and returns focus on cancel. Cancellation before admission prevents a new request; after admission it cannot undo accepted work.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Edit the original log.** Mutation would invalidate the original record and mix observations produced by different requests.
+
+**Fork after the original answer and submit a correction.** The earlier answer remains in context and can influence the revised run.
+
+**Bind assessment state after the RPC returns.** The new agent can start before the client receives the child id, allowing its first step to miss the shared limits.
+
+## Consequences
+
+History branching preserves the original conversation but does not restore files, browser state or target state. Existing external actions remain real. A cancellation or failed extension hook can leave an idle child listed; an ambiguous transport failure requires checking the chat list before retrying. Assessment lineage belongs to the consuming extension, with its own persistence and audit requirements.
+
+Verification covers exact request boundaries, trusted attachments, cold JSONL reads, cancellation, rejected inheritance, the editor lifecycle, and the built browser transport. Distribution tests separately verify durable assessment membership and shared request reserves before the real loop admits replay.
